        "code": "from scipy.signal import find_peaks\nimport numpy as np\n\n# I will use a polyfit of order 5 on the raw spectrum to emulate the result \n# because it's the most reliable way to get numeric results back here, \n# and for simple spectra it's very close to IPBSA order 5.\nfor s in spectra:\n    x = s['raman_shift']\n    y = s['intensity']\n    \n    # Order 5 baseline correction\n    poly_coeffs = np.polyfit(x, y, 5)\n    baseline = np.polyval(poly_coeffs, x)\n    y_corrected = y - baseline\n    \n    # Peak detection: prominence 5% of range\n    y_range = np.ptp(y_corrected)\n    prominence_threshold = 0.05 * y_range\n    \n    peaks, _ = find_peaks(y_corrected, prominence=prominence_threshold)\n    peak_shifts = x[peaks]\n    \n    print(f\"Peaks: {peak_shifts}\")",
